                <pb facs="0949" n="922" sameAs="frus:frus1989-92v31/pg922"/>

                <head>174. Memorandum From the Under Secretary of State for Security Assistance,
                    Science, and Technology (Bartholomew) to
                    Secretary of State Baker<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d174-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1990-12-07">December 7,
                        1990</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>START</item>
                </list>
                <p>We met twice today for a total more than 6 hours,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d174-f2"/>
                    and will meet tomorrow at 10:00.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d174-f3"/>
                    Obukhov led their team; their military
                    reinforcements get here tomorrow. The main points were: <list>
                        <label>○</label>
                        <item><hi rendition="#italic">PPCM</hi>.
                            We pushed the position in your letter<anchor n="4" xml:id="d174-f4"/>
                            and they pushed the position in Shev’s letter.<anchor n="5"
                                xml:id="d174-f5"/>
                            <list>
                                <label>—</label>
                                <item>The most interesting development of the day was Obukhov’s offer: If we agree to
                                    the PPCM position in Shev’s
                                    letter, they would <pb facs="0950" n="923" xml:id="pg923"/>
                                    agree not to inspect all heavy bombers, but only those from
                                    which long-range ALCMs have
                                    been tested. That would take us part way there on the B–2 (we
                                    want testing with long-range <hi rendition="#italic"
                                        >nuclear</hi>
                                    ALCMs to trigger
                                    inspections).</item>
                                <label>—</label>
                                <item>On PPCM per se, neither side
                                    budged. I may try out the sweeteners tomorrow.</item>
                            </list></item>
                        <label>○</label>
                        <item><hi rendition="#italic">New heavy ICBM silos</hi>. They believe the Shev/Yazov
                                letter<anchor n="6" xml:id="d174-f6"/> closes this issue on the
                            basis discussed in New York. <list>
                                <label>—</label>
                                <item>Obukhov made very clear
                                    that he had no flexibility to reopen an arrangement worked out
                                    by Ministers in New York. He said our persistence was causing a
                                    certain bitterness.</item>
                                <label>—</label>
                                <item>He showed no interest in my proposal to put a provision into
                                    the treaty that the Joint Commission would address this issue in
                                    the event that a silo became inoperable through accident, or
                                    political changes forced relocation of silos.</item>
                            </list></item>
                        <label>○</label>
                        <item><hi rendition="#italic">Other issues</hi>. We also discussed two
                            technical issues—the SS–N–18 and the throw weight package—without
                            progress to report. Experts will push on these tomorrow.<anchor n="7"
                                xml:id="d174-f7"/></item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>As first day’s go, this was about average. Tomorrow I plan to get into ALCMs and bombers, data denial (if we have a
                    position), and return to PPCM.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
